rs connect with Blake and  Schofield on a whole other level making  the movie all the more realistic and  emotional this filming technique isn't  typically used due to how complicated  and challenging it can be however if  properly executed it can elevate a movie  shock value while hyping up a viewer  senses using this method  Sam was able to inflict the fear  adrenaline and anxiety the soldiers were  feeling onto the viewers so while there  is a big risk to use the one-shot  filming method there is also a big  reward  but how did Sam manage to pull this off  and who helped him translate his vision  onto the screen well simply put Sam had  to start at square one he needed his  cast members to know exactly what was  going on before introducing the cameras  it took six months for the actors to  rehearse the entire movie before filming  could commence and pressing pause when  someone messed up wasn't always an  option as you can see entire scenes were  filmed in unison and actors were  instructed to stay in character until  the director yelled cut while filming  the soldiers running across the field  you can see actors bumping into each  other falling and getting back up but  while a lot of these run-ins were not  planned they accurately demonstrated the  messiness of a real-life battlefield  obviously two hours of screen time can't  be filmed into one perfect sequence so  Sam had to get creative when introducing  cuts into the film these cuts couldn't  happen midway through an action scene as  they would be easy to spot ruining the  illusion Sam fought so hard to perfect  although Sam's project was ambitious it  wasn't impossible and history would  prove this after all Alfred Hitchcock  managed to perfect the one shot sequence  in 1948 when he used it to create the  movie rope whenever a cut had to be made  the camera would circle an object be it  a tree or a building and only then could  the camera be stopped circling around an  object makes it easy to pick up where 